<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Extrema in cubic function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the function \(f(x)={@f@}\) on the closed interval \([{@p@},{@q@}]\).</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Find the coordinates of the local maximum point and the local minimum point of \(f\) in the open interval \(({@p@},{@q@})\).</p>

<p>Local maximum: \(\big(\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] \(,\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] \(\big)\) [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>Local minimum: \(\big(\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] \(,\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] \(\big)\) [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Calculate the values of \(f\) at the two endpoints of the interval.</p>

<p>\(f({@p@})=\) [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p>\(f({@q@})=\) [[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> State the \(x\)-coordinates at which \(f\) attains its absolute maximum and absolute minimum on the closed interval \([{@p@},{@q@}]\).</p>

<p>Absolute maximum attained at \(x=\) [[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans7]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>

<p>Absolute minimum attained at \(x=\) [[input:ans8]] [[validation:ans8]] [[feedback:prt6]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Worked solution</strong></p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> We first find the stationary points by solving \(f'(x)=0\).</p>

<p>Differentiating gives \[f'(x)={@diff(f,x)@}.\]</p>

<p>Solving \(f'(x)=0\), we obtain \[x={@ta1@}\quad\text{or}\quad x={@ta3@}.\]</p>

<p>Since \(f\) is a cubic with a positive leading coefficient and two distinct stationary points, the stationary point with the smaller \(x\)-coordinate is a local maximum, while the one with the larger \(x\)-coordinate is a local minimum.</p>

<p>Evaluating the function at these points gives \[f({@ta1@})={@ta2@},\qquad f({@ta3@})={@ta4@}.\]</p>

<p>Therefore, the local maximum point is \(\boxed{({@ta1@},{@ta2@})}\) and the local minimum point is \(\boxed{({@ta3@},{@ta4@})}\).</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> We evaluate the function at the endpoints of the interval:</p>

<p>\[f({@p@})={@ta5@},\qquad f({@q@})={@ta6@}.\]</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> To determine the absolute extrema on the closed interval, we compare the function values at the two stationary points and the two endpoints:</p>

<p>\[f({@p@})={@ta5@},\quad f({@ta1@})={@ta2@},\quad f({@ta3@})={@ta4@},\quad f({@q@})={@ta6@}.\]</p>

<p>The largest of these values is the absolute maximum, and the smallest is the absolute minimum.</p>

<p>Hence, the absolute maximum is attained at \(\boxed{x={@ta7@}}\), and the absolute minimum is attained at \(\boxed{x={@ta8@}}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
